3},{"t":"The Republic","p":2055,"l":3},{"t":"Book I","p":2055,"l":4},{"t":"Book II","p":2103,"l":4},{"t":"Book III","p":2150,"l":4},{"t":"Book IV","p":2203,"l":4},{"t":"Book V","p":2247,"l":4},{"t":"Book VI","p":2302,"l":4},{"t":"Book VII","p":2349,"l":4},{"t":"Book VIII","p":2393,"l":4},{"t":"Book IX","p":2437,"l":4},{"t":"Book X","p":2471,"l":4},{"t":"Timaeus","p":2518,"l":2},{"t":"Introduction and Analysis","p":2518,"l":3},{"t":"§ I","p":2530,"l":4},{"t":"§ II","p":2590,"l":4},{"t":"§ III","p":2608,"l":4},{"t":"§ IV","p":2632,"l":4},{"t":"§ V","p":2643,"l":4},{"t":"§ VI","p":2647,"l":4},{"t":"§ VII","p":2653,"l":4},{"t":"§ VIII","p":2657,"l":4},{"t":"Timaeus","p":2696,"l":3},{"t":"Critias","p":2844,"l":2},{"t":"Introduction and Analysis","p":2844,"l":3},{"t":"Critias","p":2858,"l":3},{"t":"Parmenides","p":2887,"l":2},{"t":"Introduction and Analysis","p":2887,"l":3},{"t":"Parmenides","p":2964,"l":3},{"t":"Theaetetus","p":3034,"l":2},{"t":"Introduction and Analysis","p":3034,"l":3},{"t":"On the Nature and Limits of Psychology","p":3155,"l":4},{"t":"Theaetetus","p":3183,"l":3},{"t":"Sophist","p":3308,"l":2},{"t":"Introduction and Analysis","p":3308,"l":3},{"t":"Sophist","p":3409,"l":3},{"t":"Statesman","p":3496,"l":2},{"t":"Introduction and Analysis","p":3496,"l":3},{"t":"Statesman","p":3569,"l":3},{"t":"Philebus","p":3661,"l":2},{"t":"Introduction and Analysis","p":3661,"l":3},{"t":"Philebus","p":3763,"l":3},{"t":"Laws","p":3853,"l":2},{"t":"Introduction and Analysis","p":3853,"l":3},{"t":"The Preamble","p":3912,"l":4},{"t":"Excursus on the Relation of the Laws of Plato to the Institutions of Crete and Lacedaemon and to the Laws and Constitution of Athens","p":4255,"l":4},{"t":"Laws","p":4305,"l":3},{"t":"Book I","p":4305,"l":4},{"t":"Book II","p":4350,"l":4},{"t":"Book III","p":4387,"l":4},{"t":"Book IV","p":4431,"l":4},{"t":"Book V","p":4466,"l":4},{"t":"Book VI","p":4511,"l":4},{"t":"Book VII","p":4577,"l":4},{"t":"Book VIII","p":4643,"l":4},{"t":"Book IX","p":4687,"l":4},{"t":"Book X","p":4748,"l":4},{"t":"Book XI","p":4797,"l":4},{"t":"Book XII","p":4856,"l":4},{"t":"Appendix","p":4915,"l":2},{"t":"Appendix I","p":4915,"l":3},{"t":"Lesser Hippias","p":4926,"l":4},{"t":"Introduction","p":4926,"l":5},{"t":"Lesser Hippias","p":4930,"l":5},{"t":"First Alcibiades","p":4955,"l":4},{"t":"Introduction","p":4955,"l":5},{"t":"First Alcibiades","p":4962,"l":5},{"t":"Menexenus","p":5021,"l":4},{"t":"Introduction","p":5021,"l":5},{"t":"Menexenus","p":5027,"l":5},{"t":"Appendix II","p":5056,"l":3},{"t":"Second Alcibiades","p":5058,"l":4},{"t":"Eryxias","p":5080,"l":4},{"t":"Introduction","p":5080,"l":5},{"t":"Eryxias","p":5081,"l":5}],"description":["Alfred North Whitehead once said, “the safest general characterization of the European philosophical tradition is that it consists of a series of footnotes to Plato.”","It’s hard to overstate Plato’s influence on the West’s philosophical heritage and its civilization. As the first philosopher whose works survived to the present day, his writings and ideas are often characterized as the starting point of Western philosophy. Nor was his influence confined to the modern form of philosophy—Plato also affected political, religious, and spiritual thinkers, including early Christian theologians.","Plato’s works are written as dramatic dialogues. His focus is often on following the argument itself—the “dialectic”—rather than working toward a specific conclusion. His mentor, Socrates, is frequently the principal speaker, but scholars still debate whether Plato was expressing Socrates’ views or merely using Socrates as a mouthpiece for his own ideas.","In general, there are forty-five major works attributed to Plato, and all but one are dialogues. Modern scholars agree that roughly half of those were definitely written by him, some of those are definitely forgeries, and the rest they’re still unsure about. In this translation Jowett includes all but one of the works that modern scholars agree are authentic, along with an appendix of selected spurious dialogues. Over time, opinion on which works attributed to Plato were definitely written by him has changed; the only work that modern scholars believe is authentic that Jowett doesn’t include in this collection is “Hippias Major.”"]}